    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity CAGR is unusable: book value was gutted by the FY2018 $22.6B tender/recap (equity $31B -> $0.8B) and is rebuilding from that floor, so the 5y equity CAGR (28.4%) diverges >30% from revenue (13.5%) and EPS (2.0%). EPS is distorted by the FY2018 TCJA charge and the FY2025 ~$5.7B deferred-tax charge. Owner-FCF CAGRs (25-31%) start from depressed FY2020/FY2022 working-capital troughs. Through-cycle 10y revenue CAGR (5.8%) is the honest anchor.",
    "notes": "Owner FCF = FCF - SBC - equity-award withholding taxes: FY20 $2,848M, FY22 $4,007M, FY23 $6,844M, FY24 $7,581M, FY25 $8,922M. Owner-FCF CAGRs run above reported because SBC+withholding grew slower than FCF over FY22-25, i.e. SBC is NOT outgrowing cash flow -- but SBC+withholding is ~9-10% of revenue, a large standing charge. Revenue 3y CAGR ~0% (FY22 peak $44.2B -> FY25 $44.3B) captures a full handset down-and-up cycle."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Apple's in-house modem finishes displacing Qualcomm across the iPhone line by FY27, taking several billion of QCT handset revenue; Android flagship share (Samsung, Xiaomi, Oppo/Vivo) holds against MediaTek, and global smartphone units stay flat. Automotive (Snapdragon Digital Chassis backlog) and IoT/PC compound at double digits, and the AI200/AI250 data-center business ships into HUMAIN but lands at a fraction of management's $5B/$15B targets -- enough, with auto, to replace the Apple hole and return revenue to mid-single-digit growth from FY29. QTL royalties stay stable at ~$5.5-6B. Margins recover from the FY26 investment trough but settle below the FY24-25 level on mix.",
    "bull": "Data center AI becomes a real third leg: HUMAIN's 200MW plus at least one hyperscaler win take data-center revenue into the mid-single-digit billions by FY29, Alphawave connectivity IP attaches to custom silicon, and the Arm judgment lets Oryon CPUs win share in Windows PCs. Automotive reaches the ~$8B+ pipeline management points to, the handset business benefits from an on-device-AI upgrade cycle, and operating leverage on the now-built opex base lifts adj EBITDA margin back above 38%. Revenue compounds high-single-digit for three years.",
    "bear": "The Apple loss lands at once while a China-led smartphone downturn (memory-cost inflation, MediaTek and in-house Chinese silicon) shrinks the Android handset pool; the AI200/AI250 racks fail to win beyond HUMAIN against Nvidia, AMD and hyperscaler ASICs, leaving a costly opex build stranded. A renewed antitrust or licensee challenge to 'no license, no chips' trims QTL rates. Revenue falls ~9% over two years and recovers only at GDP-like rates; margins stay near the FY26 trough."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "QCOM is valued as a cyclical fabless semiconductor plus a stable licensing annuity on a strict owner-FCF basis: SBC and the cash taxes paid on net-share settlement (~10% of revenue combined) are charged in the flows and the share count held flat. The FY26 trough (revenue -3%, margins compressed by AI/data-center opex) is not carried forward; margins recover toward but not back to the FY24-25 mix. The terminal value is capped at a cyclical 14x (16x bull, 12x bear) rather than a growth-tech 20x. The data-center business is treated as an option realised mainly in the bull case.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Apple modem attrition faster or deeper than base (largest historical QCT customer)",
      "AI200/AI250 data-center revenue fails to scale beyond HUMAIN, stranding the opex build",
      "Antitrust/licensee challenge to the QTL royalty model",
      "China smartphone demand and MediaTek/in-house Chinese silicon share gains",
      "SBC + withholding treatment: the street basis (SBC as non-cash) values QCOM materially higher; the house figure depends on charging ~$4.5B/yr of equity comp",
      "Cash tax: OBBBA R&D expensing and FDII changes could keep cash tax below 19% for longer (upside)"
    ]
  },
